## What I did

Turned scripts/bench/humaneval_one.sh from a one-off (hardcoded HumanEval/0) into a real adapter parameterized by problem ID — the retreat-size step toward "run a small set" without another rewrite.

The change:
1. **First positional arg selects the problem*, defaulting to `has_close_elements` — so no-arg runs behave exactly* as before (backward compat).
2. Per-problem data lives in a `case` lookup: each branch sets ENTRY_POINT (the function the scorer looks up) and reads the prompt body from a quoted heredoc. Added a second, proven problem — HumanEval/53 add(x, y) — so the parameterization isn't theoretical.
3. **Canonical checks moved into a CHECKS dict** in the Python scorer, keyed by entry point, with the entry point passed as an argv (not interpolated into the source — keeps the quoted <<'PY' heredoc clean).
4. **Unknown problem names fail loudly** — print the available list and exit non-zero (Day 124 vacuous-test wisdom: never silently pass). This check runs before the API-key gate, so it fails clearly even without a key.

Verified offline/deterministically (the whole point — no API needed for the scoring half):
• no-args → graceful ANTHROPIC_API_KEY message, exit 1
• add → selects the new problem
• nonsense_problem → clear error listing has_close_elements add, exit 1
• extracted the scorer and ran it against good/wrong/syntax-error stubs for both problems: correct → all checks passed, wrong → precise diff message + exit 1, syntax error → load failure + exit 1
• heredoc linter clean, bash -n clean, cargo fmt/clippy/build/test all green (88 passed)

Docs updated: benchmarks.md now documents the positional argument, the supported-problems table (0 and 53), the "add a problem" pattern (case branch + `AVAILABLE_PROBLEMS + CHECKS` entry), and the honest-status list gained the parameterization checkmark with batch-running as the next ⬜.

I reverted an incidental fmt cleanup in commands_spawn.rs to keep the commit scoped to the task.
